From b0c5821b14dadf1afc85c8c34ef1dda05bd7b60c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: jjuvonen-amd <joni.juvonen@amd.com>
Date: Mon, 2 Nov 2026 12:54:29 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [ROCm] symm_mem: fused all-gather + matmul and matmul +
 reduce-scatter on SDMA engines (prototype)

For large inputs, fused_all_gather_matmul and fused_matmul_reduce_scatter copy
shards out of peers' symmetric-memory buffers with tensor copies. On ROCm, HIP
runs concurrent copies from those buffers one at a time, so the all-gather
moves at about one link's rate, and the copies compete with the matmuls for
compute units.

This adds an opt-in ROCm path (TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ENABLE_ROCM_SDMA=1). Every rank
pushes its data into each peer's inbox with ROCr's
hsa_amd_memory_async_copy_on_engine: one SDMA engine per peer (the engine ROCr
prefers for writing to that peer), all peers at once, in pieces, each piece's
matmul starting as soon as it has landed. A spare engine writes a per-sender
flag once a peer's piece has landed; a one-thread kernel waits for the flags,
bounded by a timeout, and another releases the copies after a barrier. Rows
land in natural order, so results, return_A and output strides match the
default path.

By default (TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ROCM_SDMA_INBOX=ipc) each rank's inbox is plain
device memory that peers map with hipIpc handles, and the barrier is a
one-thread kernel on arrival words mapped the same way, bounded like the flag
waits; the path never rendezvouses symmetric memory, which on some amdgpu 6.x
drivers moves the memory to the host. TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ROCM_SDMA_INBOX=symm_mem
uses the group's symmetric-memory workspace and its barrier instead.

The fp8 variants, fused_all_gather_scaled_matmul and
fused_scaled_matmul_reduce_scatter, take the same path with tensor-wise or
row-wise scales. A push carries several copies of their own sizes under one
flag, so row-wise scales sharded with A land with A's rows. _scaled_mm takes
matrices only, so each piece runs one _scaled_mm per rank or per owner.

Each call picks its pieces per rank from its size: the most, up to 8, that
keep a piece at least 16 MiB (TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ROCM_SDMA_CHUNKS overrides).

Results are deterministic: the reduce-scatter sums each piece's partial sums in
rank order, in fp32, so reruns are bitwise equal.

Captured calls issue their copies from a host-function node on every replay.
Unsupported cases (last-dim gather/scatter, a scaled reduce-scatter of an A
reshaped with another scatter dim, unvalidated architectures unless
TORCH_SYMM_MEM_ROCM_SDMA_ALLOW_UNTESTED=1) take the default path.

The transport (_rocm_sdma.hip) is built with torch.utils.cpp_extension on
first use and called through ctypes; a C++ port under
torch/csrc/distributed/c10d/symm_mem is the follow-up for review.

